Now I need to understand the vulnerability. The issue is a "Stack-buffer-overflow READ" in the ssml-fuzzer. Let me look at the `GetSsmlAttribute` function more carefully - I noticed it accesses `pw[-1]` which could be a problem:

```c
static wchar_t *GetSsmlAttribute(wchar_t *pw, const char *name)
{
        int ix;
        static wchar_t empty[1] = { 0 };

        while (*pw != 0) {
                if (iswspace(pw[-1])) {
```

The `pw[-1]` access when `pw` points to the beginning of the buffer would be an out-of-bounds read. Let me check how `px` is set in `ProcessSsmlTag`:

In `ProcessSsmlTag`, `px = &xml_buf[ix]` where `ix` is the index after the tag name. If the tag name is empty (e.g., `<>`), then `ix` would be 0 and `px` would point to the start of `xml_buf`. Then `GetSsmlAttribute(px, ...)` would access `pw[-1]` which is out of bounds.

Wait, but let me check - `xml_buf` is a `wchar_t` array. Let me look at how it's declared in readclause.c:

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<parameter=command>grep -n "xml_buf\|N_XML_BUF" /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/espeak-ng/src/libespeak-ng/readclause.c | head -20</｜｜DSML｜｜>

